Thanks, I am ok with this.

But I am not sure I understand the overall sentiment. KIPs are supposed 
to be completed by feature freeze, so if we want to give more time for 
KIPs to get completed, we should have extended feature freeze deadling 
instead (and we already effectively gave two more weeks, so we adding a 
third one now)

And we should use the time between feature freeze and code freeze to 
revert changes of non-completed KIPs as necessary (ie, public API 
changes or similar -- we opened two PR just today now that we have a 4.4 
branch from our side)

Since feature freeze, actually only bug-fixes and minor KIP follow ups 
[of completed KIPs] and new tests should get merged into AK 4.4 branch, 
to stabilize the release branch before code freeze.

After code freeze, we should only accept blockers, not regular bug-fixes 
or minor improvements any longer. Maybe there is some confusion about 
how the deadlines are intended to work?


-Matthias

