Hi Lijun,

My random thoughts to resolve the problem:

1. Using snapshot as described in the alternative A. The snapshot solution
makes sure all keyless records are compacted and only the latest state for
a segmentId (or using other keys) is kept. My high-level thought is that
for each partition in __remote_log_metadata topic, we keep the latest state
for each "key" (ex: segmentId) in memory. And writing out to a file
(CURRENT_OFFSET.checkpoint) periodically. That means the logs before
CURRENT_OFFSET can be safely deleted without worrying about data loss.
Adopting this method means the migration is not necessary and we can keep
using keyless because the log compaction is done via snapshotting. You can
refer to KIP-630 to learn how KRaft metadata log is snapshotted.

2. Re-writing all the keyless records into keyed records. After completion,
we're safe to automatically change the topic into a compacted topic because
we know all the keyless records are converted to keyed records now.

3. Adopting the current solution in the KIP, but in the background, we need
to periodically check if the keyless records are safe to be deleted. For
example, in the __remote_log_metadata-0 partition, we have 2 segments, one
is inactive, and one is active. So we scan all the records in the inactive
segment, and filter out the keyed records. For each keyless record, we
check if the mapping remote log segment is deleted. If all of the remote
log segments are deleted, then we are safe to delete the log segment. This
way, we don't care how users change the retention.ms/bytes configs, we can
always make sure the deleted log segments in the __remote_log_metadata
topic are no longer used anymore.

These are just my random thoughts. Each method has pros and cons. There
should be more good solutions out there. You can also try to think more
about it. Hope it helps.

Luke


